---
/** PREVIEW ONLY — the drafts kept for comparison after CIII "Aperture XLIV"
 *  was applied to the site. Everything else in this folder was removed. */
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import { total } from '../../data/composition';

const options = [
  {
    k: '08', href: '/preview/helix', name: 'Helix', line: 'A spiral receding into the dark. — kept',
    what: 'Works wound around an axis; position on the helix is position in composition.ts.',
    live: 'Scrolling is the camera travelling down the axis, with the name held still in the middle.',
    against: 'The most cinematic, and true 3D.',
  },
  {
    k: '10', href: '/preview/deck', name: 'Deck', line: 'Cards arriving out of the dark. — kept',
    what: 'Works stacked along the axis of view, overlapping as they pass.',
    live: 'Scrolling pulls the deck through; the nearest work is always on top and named.',
    against: 'The softest and most atmospheric.',
  },
];
---

<Base title="Preview — the drafts kept" description="Preview only.">
  <div class="ix">
    <header class="ix__head">
      <p class="mono ix__eyebrow">Preview · not wired to the site</p>
      <h1 class="display ix__h1">The seven kept</h1>
      <p class="ix__lede">
        CIII “Aperture XLIV” is now the site itself, and its opening is flown down Helix's axis.
        What remains here are the seven drafts kept for comparison — five one-page treatments and
        the two 3D catalogues. All of them run on the real {total} works, the real stills and the
        real derived numbering. Every other draft has been removed.
      </p>
    </header>

    <section class="ix__drafts">
      <p class="mono ix__lbl">One page</p>
      <div class="ix__pair ix__pair--1">
        <a class="dr" href="/preview/one-civ-aperture-xlv">
          <span class="mono dr__k">CIV</span>
          <span class="display dr__n">Aperture XLV · Sentence Case</span>
          <span class="dr__t">XLIV borrowed V Corners' whole masthead; this borrows only the case. The name stays stacked and stays XL's own — two lines at 108px — and .fr__cap keeps everything it had: 31.68px on a 34.848px line, the 28ch measure, the 17.28px it sits under the name. All that changes is that it stops shouting. Nothing else moves, and that is measurable rather than assumed: the measure is set in ch, the width of the zero glyph, which does not know about text-transform, so it stays 493.194px; the line count is fixed by the 22-character re-wrap running on the source string, also blind to case; and two line boxes are a function of line-height, not of which glyphs sit in them. Measured, the frame is 389.984px in both and all four corner labels land within 0.01px of XL's. The accent was never uppercase — .hot already carries text-transform: none so “film,” could stay lowercase italic against shouting neighbours; with the neighbours quiet that rule now does nothing visible, and is left alone because it belongs to .hot everywhere on the page rather than to this one caption.</span>
        </a>
      </div>
      <div class="ix__pair ix__pair--1">
        <a class="dr" href="/preview/one-iv-monogram">
          <span class="mono dr__k">IV</span>
          <span class="display dr__n">Monogram</span>
          <span class="dr__t">The reference's central move: the two names stepped across the measure at 272px, line-height 0.82, corners carrying the small print and nothing in between. The name drifts up slower than the page as you leave it.</span>
        </a>
      </div>
      <div class="ix__pair ix__pair--1">
        <a class="dr" href="/preview/one-vi-marquee">
          <span class="mono dr__k">VI</span>
          <span class="display dr__n">Marquee</span>
          <span class="dr__t">The name as a moving band, so the first thing that happens is motion rather than a static card. Two identical copies and a −50% track, so the loop has no seam. Pauses when pointed at.</span>
        </a>
      </div>
      <div class="ix__pair ix__pair--1">
        <a class="dr" href="/preview/one-xiii-weave">
          <span class="mono dr__k">XIII</span>
          <span class="display dr__n">Weave</span>
          <span class="dr__t">The works run through the name. One card covers UGAM, another slides under KHETANI, a third sits over everything — measured, 16% of one word hidden by pictures while the other word covers 37% of the picture beneath it.</span>
        </a>
      </div>
      <div class="ix__pair ix__pair--1">
        <a class="dr" href="/preview/one-xxiv-ticket">
          <span class="mono dr__k">XXIV</span>
          <span class="display dr__n">Ticket</span>
          <span class="dr__t">Every movement is a document: perforated rule above and below, a stub down the left with number, name, folio and a seal whose text is set on a curve. The seal turns 240° as its movement crosses the window. Even the lists rule with dashes.</span>
        </a>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="ix__drafts">
      <p class="mono ix__lbl">The catalogue in three dimensions</p>
      <p class="ix__txt">
        Helix is the one the site's opening now comes from — the spiral, and the camera travelling
        down its axis. Deck is kept beside it as the other reading of the same dark: cards arriving
        out of it rather than wound around it.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="ix__drafts">
      <p class="mono ix__lbl">Work page · Aasha — three redesigns</p>
      <p class="ix__txt">
        The work page rebuilt in the site's own language: the corner labels, the mono furniture,
        the catalogue numbering, the Fraunces accent and the stills' own drift on hover. All three
        read from src/content/works/aasha.md and invent nothing — the same title, year, category,
        role, director, awards, quote, description and eight frames in all three. The live
        /work/aasha is untouched.
      </p>
      <div class="ix__pair ix__pair--3">
        <a class="dr" href="/preview/work-i-dossier">
          <span class="mono dr__k">I</span>
          <span class="display dr__n">Dossier</span>
          <span class="dr__t">The work as a file in the catalogue. It opens exactly as a movement of the homepage does — four corner labels, the middle given to the name — and then does what the homepage never can: stays on one work long enough to say something. Editorial by three borrowings from print rather than from the web: the standfirst is the largest body text on the page, because it is the only writing on it; the eight frames are a contact sheet, numbered and uniform and in shooting order, which is how a film's stills are actually looked at; and the credits are a ruled ledger, not a row of chips. The quote is the one piece of display writing and is never repeated.</span>
        </a>
        <a class="dr" href="/preview/work-ii-spread">
          <span class="mono dr__k">II</span>
          <span class="display dr__n">Spread</span>
          <span class="dr__t">Two columns on a gutter with a rule down the middle of it — the enquiry's own arrangement, borrowed because it is the one structure on this site that already reads as print. The left column is the masthead and it stays: number, title, role, director, awards, all in view while you read and while you watch. The right column is the article, in the order a print feature runs. The rule is centred by subtracting the gutter before splitting the columns, not by taking a fraction of the whole width — fr units divide what is left after the gap, and the naive version sat 3px light of the middle. Stills stagger in two files so the eye travels rather than scans.</span>
        </a>
        <a class="dr" href="/preview/work-iii-sequence">
          <span class="mono dr__k">III</span>
          <span class="display dr__n">Sequence</span>
          <span class="dr__t">The frames carry the page. Where the other two file the stills under the writing, here each one takes the full measure in order and the three pieces of writing this work has are dealt between them — the quote after the first frame, because a claim about process lands harder once you have seen a frame of what it produced. A fixed folio in the margin reports which frame owns the window, the same job the homepage's works readout does and the same treatment. Written with an IntersectionObserver first and replaced: an observer is the textbook answer and delivers nothing at all in the preview pane, so it reads eight rects against the viewport's middle on a throttled scroll instead — the same thing the homepage's own clock does, and provable.</span>
        </a>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="ix__drafts">
      <p class="mono ix__lbl">Work page · Aasha — three further redesigns</p>
      <p class="ix__txt">
        Where I–III are magazine shapes, these three each take a different printed object as their
        model — a spotting sheet, an overture, a monograph — and let that decide the layout rather
        than decorating one. Same rule as before: every field is read from
        src/content/works/aasha.md and the live /work/aasha is untouched.
      </p>
      <div class="ix__pair ix__pair--3">
        <a class="dr" href="/preview/work-iv-cue">
          <span class="mono dr__k">IV</span>
          <span class="display dr__n">Cue Sheet</span>
          <span class="dr__t">The page as the document the work was actually made from. A composer meets a film as a spotting session and the sheet that comes out of it, so the eight frames are cues hung off a single spine, alternating across it — which makes the rule a centre rather than a margin, gives every frame a facing page of empty ground, and sends the eye over the spine eight times on the way down, the reason a printed spread beats a scroll. The spine fills in accent as the sheet is read, the enquiry's own gutter rule doing the same job on a different document; the fill is derived from the sheet's box rather than the page's, so it reads 0 before the cues and 1 after them and can never drift from what is on screen. Measured: cues alternate LRLRLRLR, spine dead-centre, fill monotonic 0 to 1.</span>
        </a>
        <a class="dr" href="/preview/work-v-overture">
          <span class="mono dr__k">V</span>
          <span class="display dr__n">Overture</span>
          <span class="dr__t">The page does what the work does. Aasha's score was built before the theme — harmonic environment, textural palette, the sense of space, with the tune emerging from inside it — and the quote says so outright. So this page begins before the image arrives: the first screen is that sentence alone at 89.6px on empty ground, no still, no title, nothing but a folio. Verified: zero images on the first screen. The name comes second, the film third, the frames last and deliberately small, because by then their job is evidence rather than spectacle. The accent is carried by one word, “begins”, matched out of the content file rather than retyped, so if the quote ever changes the line simply sets plain instead of the page inventing a sentence. It costs four seconds where a still would cost one — the right trade for a page reached from a catalogue, where the visitor has already chosen this work.</span>
        </a>
        <a class="dr" href="/preview/work-vi-plate">
          <span class="mono dr__k">VI</span>
          <span class="display dr__n">Plate</span>
          <span class="dr__t">A monograph rather than a feature. An asymmetric measure with a standing outer margin, and everything that is not the work lives out there — role, director, festivals, plate numbers — set small and quiet so the eye runs down the text without stepping over apparatus, and so the apparatus is always in the same place when it is wanted. The exact opposite of the caption-under-the-picture the web defaults to. Only the plates bleed past the column: the measure is set for reading, the pictures for looking, and that one deliberate difference in width is what makes a book feel designed rather than poured. The opening paragraph takes a raised initial, not a drop cap — a dropped letter needs a float and a hand-tuned line count and breaks the moment the measure reflows. The margin folds under 1100px, where a standing margin stops being a margin and becomes a column of orphans.</span>
        </a>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="ix__drafts">
      <p class="mono ix__lbl">Work page · Aasha — three that move</p>
      <p class="ix__txt">
        Motion-led, and each one bound to scroll rather than to a timer: the page moves only while
        the reader does, reverses on the way back, and can never be mid-animation while the reader
        is still — which is what separates these from a stagger that looks right once and wrong
        every time after. All three run one clock and derive every moving thing from it, so nothing
        can drift out of step with anything else. All three hand reduced motion a complete page.
      </p>
      <div class="ix__pair ix__pair--3">
        <a class="dr" href="/preview/work-vii-aperture">
          <span class="mono dr__k">VII</span>
          <span class="display dr__n">Aperture</span>
          <span class="dr__t">The site's strongest idea, turned on one work for the first time. A fixed mat opens from a letterbox to nearly the full frame as you travel through the gallery, with the eight stills dissolving one into the next behind it — one element with a 100vmax spread shadow in the ground colour, so the frame is the shadow and there is no join to keep in register at any size. Each still's opacity is a triangle around its own slot, so two are always mixed: a film dissolves, it does not cut to black between shots. Measured at 1280×720 — the mat opens 666×230 to 1178×634, the clock runs 0 to 1 exactly, the counter walks 01 to 08, and two layers are lit through the middle of the run against one at each end.</span>
        </a>
        <a class="dr" href="/preview/work-viii-strip">
          <span class="mono dr__k">VIII</span>
          <span class="display dr__n">Strip</span>
          <span class="dr__t">The work runs sideways — eight frames on one continuous strip pulled past a gate while the page scrolls down. Turning the axis is usually a gimmick and earns its place here for one reason: a film is a strip, so the gesture is the subject rather than an effect applied to it. The travel is measured from the track rather than guessed — its own width less one screen — so the first frame starts flush at the left margin and the last lands flush at the right at every viewport and frame count. Verified: travel 5491px against a 6771px track in a 1280px window, and at the end the last frame's right edge sits at 1203, which is the same 77px margin the first one started from. The name is held still over it in difference blend.</span>
        </a>
        <a class="dr" href="/preview/work-ix-score">
          <span class="mono dr__k">IX</span>
          <span class="display dr__n">Score</span>
          <span class="dr__t">The writing arrives the way the score did — a word at a time, out of the dark, in order — so the reader watches the statement being built rather than being handed one, and each section's rule draws itself across the measure as its heading arrives. Words, not letters: splitting to characters is the showier version and it stops a word being a word to a screen reader, to find-in-page and to text selection. The split has to be written on one line, too — across several, the fragment emits its own indentation as text, it lands on top of the explicit separator, and every gap becomes two spaces; measured, the quote came out as “A  score  begins…”, invisible on screen because HTML collapses whitespace and wrong in copied text, in a screen reader's pause, and to any extractor. Both paragraphs now read back character-for-character from the content file.</span>
        </a>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="ix__drafts">
      <p class="mono ix__lbl">Work page · Aasha — IX rebuilt</p>
      <p class="ix__txt">
        Same spine in all three, and it is the fix IX needed most: <b>what is this · what did he do
        on it · let me see it · why it matters · the details</b>. IX led with a statement, put the
        film third, and set the role — the single most important fact on a composer's work page —
        in 10px of mono metadata. Here it is display type under the title. The two weakest sections
        are rebuilt in each: the awards line and the credits.
      </p>
      <p class="ix__txt">
        <b>The tally is parsed, not retyped.</b> The content file holds one string —
        “7 Official Selections · 10 Nominations · 6 Wins” — and IX printed it as a line of 11px
        caps, so three hard-won numbers read as a caption. Split into figure/label pairs, the
        numbers can be set as numbers. If any part does not begin with a figure the whole tally
        falls back to the original line, so a differently shaped award can never render broken —
        and in all three, re-joining the parsed pairs gives the source string character-for-character.
      </p>
      <div class="ix__pair ix__pair--3">
        <a class="dr" href="/preview/work-x-billing">
          <span class="mono dr__k">X</span>
          <span class="display dr__n">Billing</span>
          <span class="dr__t">The tally as three display figures on a ruled band, centred, with the labels in mono beneath — measured, 112px against IX's 11px caps. The credits become a billing block: the stack at the foot of a film poster, centred and caps and strictly hierarchical, role largest, director under it, specifications smallest. It suits a short film better than a table does and closes the page the way the thing itself closes. No condensed face is pretended — a real billing block is set in compressed gothic, this site does not load one, and --display falls back to Helvetica, so the effect is built from caps, tight tracking and centred hierarchy rather than from a face that would silently not arrive.</span>
        </a>
        <a class="dr" href="/preview/work-xi-ledger">
          <span class="mono dr__k">XI</span>
          <span class="display dr__n">Ledger</span>
          <span class="dr__t">The page set as a document rather than a poster — the whole reading ranged left. The tally reads left to right like a line of accounts, the figure in its own column with the label beside it rather than beneath, so the three rows scan as the quantities they are. The credits become a grouped spec sheet: IX gave one flat run of six rows with nothing to say that a director and a format are different KINDS of fact, so they are split under their own ruled subheads — Production, Recognition, Elsewhere — and the block can be scanned for the one thing wanted instead of read through. Verified: three groups, six rows, three, and one.</span>
        </a>
        <a class="dr" href="/preview/work-xii-card">
          <span class="mono dr__k">XII</span>
          <span class="display dr__n">Card</span>
          <span class="dr__t">Both sections set in the panel the rest of the site already uses — same 1px rule, same 14px radius, same ground the homepage gives its enquiry — so the foot of the page reads as one object rather than two adjacent lists. Each figure gets a ruled row with a leader running out to it, the way a results table sets a score, so the three land on a common right edge and compare at a glance: measured, all three figures flush at 1159px. The leader is a flexed hairline rather than a row of dots, because dots do not sit on a baseline reliably across faces. The role is lifted out of the rows and set as display type at the head of the colophon, because it is the answer to the only question the page exists to answer.</span>
        </a>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="ix__drafts">
      <p class="mono ix__lbl">Work page · Aasha — IX rebuilt, second set</p>
      <p class="ix__txt">
        Same brief as X–XII, kept alongside them. The spine is the same fix — role as display type
        under the title, film early, details last — and the tally is parsed from the file's single
        string the same way, falling back to the raw line if any part does not begin with a figure.
        What differs here is <b>where recognition sits</b>, which is a layout question rather than
        a styling one, and each of the three answers it differently.
      </p>
      <div class="ix__pair ix__pair--3">
        <a class="dr" href="/preview/work-xiii-prose">
          <span class="mono dr__k">XIII</span>
          <span class="display dr__n">Prose</span>
          <span class="dr__t">Recognition folds into the writing: a festival record is a claim about the work, so it belongs in the paragraph that makes claims about the work rather than in a band of its own three screens later. The figures are set at display size inline in that sentence — 48px, still part of a line of prose, with vertical-align keeping the numeral on the same baseline as the words beside it. The credits run as prose too, which film writing has always done: values in bone, labels dim, the two colours doing the work a table's rules would. And prose cannot repeat itself the way a table can — a table may carry Year and Format beside a heading that states them and nobody notices, but as a sentence it came out “…a short film directed by Sameer Talawdekar, 2025. Format Short film. Year 2025.” Any spec already said in the lead is now dropped, matched on the value rather than the label so a file can name that field anything.</span>
        </a>
        <a class="dr" href="/preview/work-xiv-backpage">
          <span class="mono dr__k">XIV</span>
          <span class="display dr__n">Back Page</span>
          <span class="dr__t">Recognition and credits become one closing spread. Both are apparatus — neither is the work — so rather than two separate bands with a gallery between them, the page ends on a single back page holding both: the record as a column of figures on the left, the credits ranged left beside it. It leaves the body of the page pure, with nothing between the title, the film, the writing and the stills but the work itself, and puts everything a reader might want to look up afterwards in the one place they will look. The figures are stacked rather than rowed so they can sit at headline size without three of them fighting for a line — measured, all three on a common left edge at 77px.</span>
        </a>
        <a class="dr" href="/preview/work-xv-table">
          <span class="mono dr__k">XV</span>
          <span class="display dr__n">Table</span>
          <span class="dr__t">Recognition sits directly under the film, because that is the moment it answers a question the reader has just formed: you watch, you wonder whether it landed, and the record is the next thing on the page. Both sections are set as a real ruled table on a strict grid — the plainest of the six and the most exact. Three figures in three cells of equal width compare instantly, measured at 375px each, and a credit grid with visible cells cannot develop the ambiguous middle column a borderless list does at narrow widths. One hairline per edge: the table draws its own left and top, each cell its right and bottom — drawn on all four, every internal edge doubles and reads at 1px on a dark ground as uneven weight rather than as a heavier rule.</span>
        </a>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="ix__drafts">
      <p class="mono ix__lbl">Work page · Aasha — the assembled sequence</p>
      <p class="ix__txt">
        The order you specified, identical in all three: <b>the film · the title · the statement ·
        the credits · the writing · the recognition · the stills</b>. Each section is the treatment
        from the draft it came from — the statement is IX's, assembling word by word on scroll; the
        credits are XV's ruled table; the writing and the recognition band are X's. What varies is
        only the first two: how the film opens the page, and how the title is set against it.
        Credits before the writing is unusual and deliberate — it answers “who did what” while the
        film is still in mind.
      </p>
      <div class="ix__pair ix__pair--3">
        <a class="dr" href="/preview/work-xvi-screening">
          <span class="mono dr__k">XVI</span>
          <span class="display dr__n">Screening</span>
          <span class="dr__t">The film runs edge to edge at the very top with nothing above it, because it is what the visitor came for and the page has nothing to say first. The title follows underneath at full size, centred, role in accent below it. Breaking the margin is done with a negative inline margin rather than 100vw: 100vw includes the scrollbar gutter on every desktop engine that reserves one, so a 100vw child inside a padded page overflows by exactly the scrollbar and gives the document a horizontal scroll — cancelling the padding with its own negative margin cannot, because it is the same number. Measured: the opening sits at left 0, width 1280 in a 1280 viewport, with zero horizontal overflow at every scroll depth.</span>
        </a>
        <a class="dr" href="/preview/work-xvii-marquee">
          <span class="mono dr__k">XVII</span>
          <span class="display dr__n">Marquee</span>
          <span class="dr__t">The title lives on the film. There is no separate title block at all — the name is set across the frame in difference blend, so the two are one object the way a title card is. Difference rather than a scrim: a panel behind the type would dim the picture it sits on, where difference inverts against whatever is under it and leaves the frame untouched — visible in the letters as they cross the face. The overlay is pointer-events: none, which is structural rather than cosmetic: the facade beneath is a button, and a full-bleed label over it would swallow the play control. Verified — the title box matches the frame exactly at [0, 720], and the play control is still reachable through it.</span>
        </a>
        <a class="dr" href="/preview/work-xviii-plate">
          <span class="mono dr__k">XVIII</span>
          <span class="display dr__n">Plate</span>
          <span class="dr__t">The film is inset rather than bled — held inside the page's own measure with air around it, the way a plate sits on a page — and the title runs beneath as a two-column head: name left, facts ranged right against it on a rule. A bled frame reads as a hero and asks to be looked at before anything else is considered; a matted one reads as a plate inside a document and lets the head beside it carry equal weight, which suits a reader who wants the facts as fast as the film. The two columns meet on a baseline rather than a box, so the rule under them is continuous and the block reads as one head instead of two stacks — measured, name and facts both ending at 838px.</span>
        </a>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="ix__drafts">
      <p class="mono ix__lbl">Work page · Aasha — XVII reworked</p>
      <p class="ix__txt">
        XVII's opening is unchanged in all three — the name across the frame in difference blend,
        no scrim, the overlay pointer-events: none so the play control underneath stays reachable.
        <b>The strip beneath it is gone.</b> The folio, the role and “A film by Sameer Talawdekar”
        repeated three facts the credit table already holds, and put a block of small print between
        the film and the statement — the one place on the page where nothing should interrupt.
        Role and director are still on the page: they are in Credits, where someone looking for
        them would look. What differs across the three is the order of the score, the recognition
        and the credits.
      </p>
      <div class="ix__pair ix__pair--3">
        <a class="dr" href="/preview/work-xix-close">
          <span class="mono dr__k">XIX</span>
          <span class="display dr__n">Close</span>
          <span class="dr__t">Ordered the way a film ends: the work, then how it was received, then who made it. Credits last is not a convention for its own sake — it is the only one of the three a reader might want to look <i>up</i> rather than read through, and a section people arrive at deliberately belongs at the end where it can always be found. Verified order: film · statement · the score · recognition · credits · stills.</span>
        </a>
        <a class="dr" href="/preview/work-xx-curtain">
          <span class="mono dr__k">XX</span>
          <span class="display dr__n">Curtain</span>
          <span class="dr__t">The people before the record. The writing explains what was made, the credits say who made it, and the festival record closes — so the page ends on its strongest objective claim rather than on a table. This is the better order if the recognition is the point: where XIX treats the record as one fact among several, this treats it as the conclusion the rest of the page has been building toward, and gives it the last word before the stills.</span>
        </a>
        <a class="dr" href="/preview/work-xxi-evidence">
          <span class="mono dr__k">XXI</span>
          <span class="display dr__n">Evidence</span>
          <span class="dr__t">The record stops being its own section. A festival tally is evidence for the paragraph above it, not a separate topic, so the writing and the figures become one block — description, then the three figures directly beneath it inside the same rule. Five stops become four, and every heading is a small decision about whether to keep going; two of XVII's were answering the same question, which is what this work is and whether it landed. The band drops its top rule inside the block, because the block already carries one under its heading and two hairlines a few lines apart read as a mistake rather than as structure — measured, top 0px and bottom 1px.</span>
        </a>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="ix__drafts">
      <p class="mono ix__lbl">Work page · Aasha — IX with XV's credits and X's recognition</p>
      <p class="ix__txt">
        IX's order, animations and split are untouched in all three: head, statement, film, the
        score, stills, credits; the words still assemble on scroll; the rules still draw; the
        stills still arrive staggered by column. XV's ruled credit table and X's recognition band
        are swapped in, the band sitting exactly where IX's one-line awards caption sat so nothing
        moves. <b>What differs is the skeleton</b> — three answers to clean, minimalist and editorial.
      </p>
      <div class="ix__pair ix__pair--3">
        <a class="dr" href="/preview/work-xxii-measure">
          <span class="mono dr__k">XXII</span>
          <span class="display dr__n">Measure</span>
          <span class="dr__t">Everything on one measure. A single centred column at reading width and nothing on the page allowed to be wider than it — not the film, not the stills, not the tables — so the page has exactly two vertical edges and every element begins and ends on them. Minimalism here is a rule about width rather than about removing things. Measured: every block at 760px, one value across the whole page. The stills drop to two up and the credit table to two columns, because three cells on a 760px measure give a 240px thumbnail rather than a still, and leave no room for a value to sit on one line.</span>
        </a>
        <a class="dr" href="/preview/work-xxiii-rail">
          <span class="mono dr__k">XXIII</span>
          <span class="display dr__n">Rail</span>
          <span class="dr__t">Every section heading leaves the content column for a narrow one of its own, so the reading edge is unbroken from the first line to the last and no heading ever steps into the column to push text down — the spec-sheet move, and what makes a technical document look composed rather than listed. The drawing rule comes with it, turned ninety degrees: same custom property, same clock, drawn down the rail instead of across the measure. Getting it to measure the section took three attempts — height against the label gave a 1×0 box, stretching it covered only the first content child, and grid-row 1/-1 does nothing when every row is implicit. Making the section the containing block fixes all three: verified 504/504, 363/363, 761/761 and 242/242, all on one edge at 77px.</span>
        </a>
        <a class="dr" href="/preview/work-xxiv-plain">
          <span class="mono dr__k">XXIV</span>
          <span class="display dr__n">Plain</span>
          <span class="dr__t">The structural rules come off. No hairline under a heading, no band around the recognition, no borders between credit cells — verified zero on all three — so space does the dividing and the four rules the animation draws become the only ones that divide anything. Where every section had a rule it read as furniture; alone it reads as a mark being made. Two lines survive and should: the play control's outline and the credit links' underline, which are affordances rather than furniture and say the one thing a page with no other lines still has to say. This is the most minimal of the three and the most fragile — a page with no rules relies entirely on its spacing being right, and has nothing to hide behind if it is not.</span>
        </a>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="ix__drafts">
      <p class="mono ix__lbl">Work page · Aasha — three readings of XXIV “Plain”</p>
      <p class="ix__txt">
        XXIV's principle holds in all three: <b>no structural rules</b>. IX's order, its assembling
        words, its drawing rules and the stills' staggered arrival are untouched, and XV's credit
        table and X's recognition band stay where XXIV put them. What changes is what replaces the
        lines — a staircase, a number, or a change of ground.
      </p>
      <div class="ix__pair ix__pair--3">
        <a class="dr" href="/preview/work-xxv-ledge">
          <span class="mono dr__k">XXV</span>
          <span class="display dr__n">Ledge</span>
          <span class="dr__t">The lines become a staircase. Each section starts one --margin further in than the one before, so the left edge descends in steps and a section announces itself by where it begins rather than by a rule above it — the indent is the division, and the scale is the page's own number rather than an invented one. The measure shortens as it steps, which is the point: the column narrows as the page descends and the eye is drawn inward instead of left hanging at full width. Measured at 1280: steps of 77, 76, 77, 77 and a measure running 1126 → 1050 → 973 → 896 → 819. It flattens under 900px, where four steps of 77 would leave the last section a gutter.</span>
        </a>
        <a class="dr" href="/preview/work-xxvi-ghost">
          <span class="mono dr__k">XXVI</span>
          <span class="display dr__n">Ghost</span>
          <span class="dr__t">The lines become numbers. Each section carries its folio set enormous and almost invisible behind the heading — 166px of display type at four per cent — so it registers as a change of texture rather than as something to read. It is the only ornament on the page and it is also data: the figure is the section's position, so the one decorative element is telling the reader where they are, which is the only justification ornament on a minimal page has. Worth being exact about the weight: white at 0.04 over this ground computes to #141312 and 1.07:1, where the hairline XXIV removed was 1.37:1 — roughly a third of a rule's contrast, deliberately. Taken up to the rule's own 0.13 it stops being a ghost and competes with the heading on top of it. Absolutely positioned so it can never push the column, and aria-hidden so no screen reader announces “zero three” before every heading.</span>
        </a>
        <a class="dr" href="/preview/work-xxvii-tone">
          <span class="mono dr__k">XXVII</span>
          <span class="display dr__n">Tone</span>
          <span class="dr__t">The lines become tone. Alternate sections sit on a lifted ground edge to edge, so the page divides by value rather than by rule — the oldest way of separating one thing from another, and the one that needs no ink at all. It is a one-step change, #0A0908 to #0F0E0D: five values, enough to read as a different ground where it meets its neighbour and not enough to read as a panel sitting on the page. A minimal page can afford exactly one of these; two would be a colour scheme. The bleed is the page's own padding as a negative inset, not 100vw — that includes the scrollbar gutter and would overflow by exactly its width. And the toned sections take the padding while the gaps between blocks close, or the tone reads as a band floating in a gap rather than as a change of ground.</span>
        </a>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="ix__drafts">
      <p class="mono ix__lbl">Work page · Aasha — three more readings of XXIV “Plain”</p>
      <p class="ix__txt">
        XXIV's no-rules principle holds; IX's order and animations are untouched; XV's credit table
        and X's recognition band stay where they were. Each of these organises the page with one
        idea and adds nothing to it.
      </p>
      <div class="ix__pair ix__pair--3">
        <a class="dr" href="/preview/work-xxviii-axis">
          <span class="mono dr__k">XXVIII</span>
          <span class="display dr__n">Axis</span>
          <span class="dr__t">The whole page hangs off one invisible line at the golden section — headings ranged right into it, content ranged left away from it, and nothing ever drawn there. It is the opposite of a rail: a rail is a column with things in it, an axis is a line with things on both sides, and the type does the work of making it visible. Measured at 1280: the grid splits 413.7 / 669.2, all four heading text edges land on exactly 490 and every content block on 534. The drawing rule turns round to suit — XXIV's grows left to right from the start of a heading, but these headings run the other way, so it takes transform-origin 100% and grows toward the spine; verified, the origin resolves to the rule's own right edge. 0.382 is not decoration: a half-and-half split gives a two-word heading far more room than it needs and starves the reading.</span>
        </a>
        <a class="dr" href="/preview/work-xxix-caesura">
          <span class="mono dr__k">XXIX</span>
          <span class="display dr__n">Caesura</span>
          <span class="dr__t">Every section gets a screen of its own and sits in the middle of it. Emptiness is the most expensive material on a page and almost the only one this page has left — and a portfolio can afford it, because there are six things to say and a reader who has already chosen the work. It multiplies with the animation rather than fighting it: with a section alone on a screen the whole sentence crosses IX's reading line together, so it assembles as one gesture instead of trickling in behind a heading. Verified — the first six words share a value of 0.68, three distinct values across the paragraph where the stacked layout gives a gradient. min-height rather than height, so the stills at eight frames may exceed the screen: measured 1.58 of one, against 1.00 for every section that fits.</span>
        </a>
        <a class="dr" href="/preview/work-xxx-recto">
          <span class="mono dr__k">XXX</span>
          <span class="display dr__n">Recto</span>
          <span class="dr__t">The page set as facing leaves: sections alternate between the left and right halves, each taking one and leaving the other empty, so the reading crosses the page as it descends and the empty half is as much of the design as the full one. Nothing is ever placed opposite anything — a reader is never asked which side to read, only to follow one thing across and down. Verified LRLRL, 563px each. And it only works if the type comes down with the container: halved at XXIV's body size it set 37 characters a line, as far below the comfortable range as the full 1180px width is above it at 110. A layout that halves the container and not the type has only traded one bad measure for another. At 17.28px it now sets 53, longest line 52.</span>
        </a>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="ix__drafts">
      <p class="mono ix__lbl">Work page · Aasha — XXI refined to XXIV's discipline</p>
      <p class="ix__txt">
        XXI kept its identity — the title on the film in difference blend, the statement assembling
        word by word, the record folded into the writing as its evidence — and takes XXIV's
        bearing. <b>Credits move below the stills</b>: they are the one section a reader looks
        <i>up</i> rather than reads through, and XXI had them interrupting the run between the
        writing and the pictures. Every structural rule comes off — no hairline under a heading, no
        rule under the record, no borders in the credit table — and one line goes back, drawn
        rather than set, scaling in as its section arrives on the same clock as the words.
      </p>
      <div class="ix__pair ix__pair--2">
        <a class="dr" href="/preview/work-xxxi-quiet">
          <span class="mono dr__k">XXXI</span>
          <span class="display dr__n">Quiet</span>
          <span class="dr__t">One axis down the middle, every block hung on it, and a great deal of nothing between them — the closest of the three to XXIV's own bearing. The drawing rule grows from the middle out rather than from the left, because a centred heading with a rule opening from its left edge is a mark pointing away from everything above it; one word changed, and it now opens symmetrically under the label it belongs to. It is also short rather than full width: under a centred label a full-measure rule reads as a divider between sections, where a short one reads as an underline belonging to the words. Verified — origin resolves to 50%, rule 77px wide and centred on the label to within a pixel.</span>
        </a>
        <a class="dr" href="/preview/work-xxxiv-quiet-ghost">
          <span class="mono dr__k">XXXIV</span>
          <span class="display dr__n">Quiet · Ghost</span>
          <span class="dr__t">XXXI with XXVI's folios. Everything still centred on one axis with a great deal of nothing between the blocks, and each labelled section now carrying its number set enormous and almost invisible behind the heading — 166px of display type at four per cent, the one ornament on the page and one that is also data, since the figure is the section's position. Centred rather than left-anchored: XXVI hangs its number off the left because XXVI ranges its headings left, and on a page built on a centre axis that would be the only thing not hung on it. Three numbers, not five — the film carries the title across it and the statement is one sentence alone on the page; neither has a heading for a number to sit behind, and giving them one would mean inventing headings to justify the ornament. Verified: all three centred on both block and label, absolutely positioned and out of flow (removing one leaves the heading at exactly the same 1012px), and aria-hidden so nothing announces “zero two” before every section.</span>
        </a>
        <a class="dr" href="/preview/work-xxxii-hang">
          <span class="mono dr__k">XXXII</span>
          <span class="display dr__n">Hang</span>
          <span class="dr__t">Every heading steps out into the page margin, so the reading column has one unbroken left edge and no heading ever interrupts it — verified, body, credit cells, figures and stills all landing on 77 with the labels outdented to 15. That took a correction worth recording: XXI inherits X's centred body, so before the fix the paragraph sat at x 204 and there was no single left edge to have. The outdent is capped at a fraction of the page's own margin, never a fixed number — there is exactly that much room to the left of the column, and this project has already put a label at x −46 by ignoring it. The rule hangs with the label and stays the label's width, since one running the full measure would cross back under the text it just stepped out of.</span>
        </a>
        <a class="dr" href="/preview/work-xxxiii-beside">
          <span class="mono dr__k">XXXIII</span>
          <span class="display dr__n">Beside</span>
          <span class="dr__t">The only one of the three that changes what the block means: the evidence sits <i>beside</i> the claim rather than under it, so the sentence and the record that supports it are read in one eyeful. XXI folded the record into the writing to save a stop; this puts them on one line of sight, which is the stronger version of the same idea. The figures stack rather than row, on a common left edge to be read down while the prose is read across — three in a row beside a paragraph would compete for the same horizontal sweep. Measured: the block splits 658.7 / 403.7, figures all on 799. And the cost is stated rather than hidden — the split lands the reading at 46 characters, inside the range but at the short end, so it serves the paragraph only while the paragraph is short.</span>
        </a>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="ix__drafts">
      <p class="mono ix__lbl">Work page · Aasha — XXXI with XXVI's numbers, three ways</p>
      <p class="ix__txt">
        XXXI's bearing throughout: everything centred on one axis, deep air between the blocks, the
        statement assembling word by word, the short rule drawing from the middle out, credits below
        the stills. What differs is what the number <i>does</i> — sit behind a heading, hold a whole
        block, mark the gap between sections, or persist as one figure the page moves through.
        Four per cent computes to #141312 and 1.07:1 on this ground, against 1.37:1 for the
        hairline weight the drawn rule uses: a texture the eye takes for structure, never a mark
        competing with the type on it. All are aria-hidden.
      </p>
      <div class="ix__pair ix__pair--3">
        <a class="dr" href="/preview/work-xxxv-watermark">
          <span class="mono dr__k">XXXV</span>
          <span class="display dr__n">Watermark</span>
          <span class="dr__t">The figure leaves the heading and grows to the size of the whole block, centred behind everything in it — a section is not labelled with a number, it sits on one, the way a plate in a book sits on its page. It has to go fainter as it goes bigger: area carries weight, and a numeral covering roughly nine times the ink at the same four per cent stops being a texture and becomes a shape behind the words, so this runs at three. Measured at 384px: two digits come out 366px wide inside a 1126px measure, nowhere near clipping — worth checking rather than assuming, and the size is in vw against a measure that is a share of the same viewport, so the ratio holds as the window changes. The −0.08em tracking is there because a figure this large opens up at the default and reads as two numbers rather than one.</span>
        </a>
        <a class="dr" href="/preview/work-xxxvi-interval">
          <span class="mono dr__k">XXXVI</span>
          <span class="display dr__n">Interval</span>
          <span class="dr__t">The numbers leave the headings entirely and stand alone in the empty space between sections, so the space that was already doing the dividing now has a mark in it and the figure stops being a label and becomes a caesura. It is the only one of the four where the ornament is load-bearing: XXXIV's folio could be deleted and the page would read as before, where here the figure is what tells you a section has ended — the job XXIV gave to emptiness alone, handed something to hold. It sits on the half of its own gap, written as half the same clamp the blocks are separated by, so the placement cannot drift if the spacing changes: measured, the second and third land at exactly 0px from the gap's midpoint. And it can carry more weight than a figure behind type — six per cent, because nothing is set on top of it.</span>
        </a>
        <a class="dr" href="/preview/work-xxxvii-counter">
          <span class="mono dr__k">XXXVII</span>
          <span class="display dr__n">Counter</span>
          <span class="dr__t">One number instead of three: a single figure fixed behind the page, 435px and almost invisible, changing as you pass from one section into the next — the ornament is not repeated down the page, it is one continuous thing the page moves through. It is also the only one of the four that is navigation. A folio behind a heading tells you what you are looking at, which the heading already said; a folio that persists tells you where you are in the whole, which nothing else here says. It runs on the same clock as the words and the rule, so it cannot disagree with the animation about which section you are in — verified across all five, 01 through 05. Fixed at z-index 0 behind content at 1, and pointer-events none: a full-screen figure that could take a click would swallow the play control on the film, and the control is confirmed still reachable through it.</span>
        </a>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="ix__drafts">
      <p class="mono ix__lbl">Loading screen — Leader is live; four alternates kept</p>
      <p class="ix__txt">
<b>A · Leader is the one now running on the site.</b> The other four are kept here as
        alternates and are all <b>drop-in replacements for Opening.astro</b> — same props, same
        html.intro-on gate, same 1560ms budget — so switching to any of them is a single import
        swapped in index.astro. Each preview plays its loader on load and again on the button or
        the R key, because the real one runs once per session and is otherwise almost impossible
        to look at. The letters are the ones each draft was given when it was built, so the gaps
        are drafts that have since been dropped.
      </p>
      <div class="ix__pair ix__pair--4">
        <a class="dr" href="/preview/loading-a-leader">
          <span class="mono dr__k">A</span>
          <span class="display dr__n">Leader</span>
          <span class="dr__t">The Academy countdown: a sweep hand wipes the ring once per count while the numerals cut 3 · 2 · 1. It is the oldest opening in cinema and it does real work — a countdown is the only kind of loading screen that tells you exactly how much longer it will be. Each count holds a still from the catalogue behind the ring, so 1.1 seconds shows you three works while it counts. The sweep is one conic gradient on a registered @property angle, not twelve elements or an SVG arc — registered because an unregistered custom property has no type and the browser can only swap it, which would make the hand jump in three steps. The stills run at brightness 0.8: measured, the 0.62 this started at left a mean channel of 27.5 against a ground of 10, which is a frame that is technically there and effectively black, and the white numeral still reads 15.5:1 at the brighter grade.</span>
        </a>

        <a class="dr" href="/preview/loading-d-gate">
          <span class="mono dr__k">D</span>
          <span class="display dr__n">Gate</span>
          <span class="dr__t">Film through a projector. A strip of the catalogue is pulled up past a fixed gate with sprocket holes running down both edges, and decelerates onto one frame and stops. The name sits in the gate the whole time, so the strip runs behind it rather than beside it. The strip moves, not the frames — one translateY on the whole thing, so eight stills cost one composited layer and one animated property between them. The travel is exactly seven frame-heights, so it lands with the eighth centred: a projector coming to rest between frames is a fault and it looks like one. Measured, it stops dead centre. Two things were fixed by looking: the sprockets were in the stylesheet and not on the screen, because the opaque edge was listed as the first background layer and the first layer paints on top of the holes; and the name was set in difference, which inverts whatever is behind it, so over a warm frame it came out saturated blue. It is plain bone over a flat scrim now, and every one of the eight frames clears 6.9:1 against its own brightest pixel.</span>
        </a>

        <a class="dr" href="/preview/loading-e-contact">
          <span class="mono dr__k">E</span>
          <span class="display dr__n">Contact</span>
          <span class="dr__t">The contact sheet. The whole catalogue lands as a grid of small frames, each cut in a beat after the last and numbered in the margin the way a real sheet is, then the sheet leans toward the camera and the page is behind it. It is the only opening here that shows you everything before it shows you anything: a second of it and you already know the shape of the body of work. The stagger is the design and it is 42ms — eight at that spacing is 294ms of cascade inside a 380ms cell, so they overlap and it reads as one gesture with texture rather than as eight separate arrivals. Measured mid-run, the eight cells sit at 0.96, 0.92, 0.83, 0.66, 0.34, 0, 0, 0: a genuine cascade, not a fade. The lean at the end is one transform on the sheet, so eight frames cost one composited layer; animating the grid's own tracks would relayout the whole thing every frame.</span>
        </a>

        <a class="dr" href="/preview/loading-f-scope">
          <span class="mono dr__k">F</span>
          <span class="display dr__n">Scope</span>
          <span class="dr__t">The aspect ratio is the animation. The screen starts shut and the two mattes draw apart to exactly 2.39:1 with a frame and the name inside the slot. Nothing moves but the shape of the frame itself, and it never opens past scope — the lift takes the whole thing away instead, so it reads as a piece of film ending rather than as a curtain being cleared. Holding the ratio needed restructuring rather than a clamp: with the mattes running the full width, the slot's width is fixed, so any ceiling on its height changes the aspect, and the first attempt measured 2.581:1 at 1280×800 — a letterbox, but not scope. The stage narrows instead of the frame flattening. Measured at 1280×800 and at 1400×480, both land on exactly 2.39, and on the short one the stage sits at 1010px with ground either side, which is what a projected scope image actually looks like.</span>
        </a>

        <a class="dr" href="/preview/loading-h-slats">
          <span class="mono dr__k">H</span>
          <span class="display dr__n">Slats</span>
          <span class="dr__t">A departure board made of film. Four tall windows each run their own reel of frames upward, each stopping 60ms after the one to its left, so the catalogue lands as a cascade rather than all at once. Each reel is one transform, and the twelve slots draw from eight stills, so the columns differ from each other without costing a single extra decode. The stop is eased almost entirely into the tail, which is how a mechanical reel with mass actually comes to rest. The timing was wrong on the first build and the measurement caught it: at a 0.94s run the board settled at 1.15s and the name was at opacity 0.17 when the lift began — the entire payoff was happening off-screen. At 0.62s the reels are all at rest by 820ms and the name is fully on at 1120ms, 40ms clear of the lift.</span>
        </a>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="ix__drafts">
      <p class="mono ix__lbl">Recognition — Diwali Ke Shor Mein, three options</p>
      <p class="ix__txt">
        <b>Awaiting a decision — nothing is applied.</b> The live section renders as one
        1126px line of orange caps: <i>Official Selection · Indian Film Festival of Melbourne ·
        Official Selection · Toulouse Indian Film Festival</i>. That is not a styling choice,
        it is a parsing one — the page joins every award entry with " · " and then splits the
        result on "·", which throws away the only thing that told the entries apart. The dot
        <i>inside</i> an award becomes identical to the dot <i>between</i> awards. Fifteen works
        have a single award and never show the damage; Diwali has two, so it does. All three
        drafts keep each entry whole and split on the first dot only, which is also what keeps
        "Waves Film Bazaar 2025 · IFFI Goa" from breaking into a festival and a city. Each is
        shown in place — folio 02, under the real label and the real paragraph — with a strip
        below showing the same treatment on the other award shapes in the catalogue. The count
        band on Aasha and Broken Reflections is untouched by all three.
      </p>
      <div class="ix__pair ix__pair--3">
        <a class="dr" href="/preview/rec-a-index">
          <span class="mono dr__k">A</span>
          <span class="display dr__n">Index</span>
          <span class="dr__t">The selections as an index: one numbered row per entry, ruled off from the next, the honour set small above the festival that gave it. The festivals carry the display type because the festivals are what differ — "Official Selection" is the same on both rows, and the live page sets it just as loud as the names it repeats. The figure column is tabular and fixed, so both festivals start on one left edge (measured: 165px on each row) and the rows read as one ordered set. It is the draft that scales to a long list — the one to choose if a work ever collects a dozen selections.</span>
        </a>

        <a class="dr" href="/preview/rec-b-plate">
          <span class="mono dr__k">B</span>
          <span class="display dr__n">Plate</span>
          <span class="dr__t">The band the page already has, given a hierarchy. Same centred shape, same rules top and bottom, same place at the foot of section 02 — only the inside changes. Each selection becomes a title-card stack: the honour in small accent caps, the festival in the display face beneath. The two entries are divided by a short centred rule rather than by the same dot used inside them, which is the specific thing that makes the live version read as one run-on sentence. The smallest of the three moves — nothing about the page's rhythm changes, so it can go in without anything else being reconsidered. Centred stacks stop scanning past three or four, so this is the draft for a short list.</span>
        </a>

        <a class="dr" href="/preview/rec-c-register">
          <span class="mono dr__k">C</span>
          <span class="display dr__n">Register</span>
          <span class="dr__t">The honour stated once. Both of Diwali's awards are Official Selections, so identical citations collapse into a single heading and the festivals are filed beneath it — the section says "Official Selection" once, in the accent, then names the two festivals. It carries a figure, which is how the rest of the site already counts: Aasha and Broken Reflections set their recognition as big tabular numerals, and this puts Diwali in the same voice at a smaller scale, so two selections and seven read as the same kind of statement. It is also the only one of the three that scales in both directions — one citation with two bodies, five citations with one each, or a bare award with no body at all. Its limit, plainly: grouping only earns anything while citations repeat, which on the fourteen single-award works means it collapses to what Index would have done anyway.</span>
        </a>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="ix__drafts">
      <p class="mono ix__lbl">Mosaic bed — twelve works, ranked, three options</p>
      <p class="ix__txt">
        <b>Awaiting a decision — nothing is applied.</b> Each page reproduces the real viewing
        conditions: the aperture mat, panels at their real widths, and the scroll clock driving
        <i>--lit</i> and <i>--prog</i>. Two buttons on each — <b>hide panels</b> and <b>show cell
        map</b> — so any claim about what is visible can be checked rather than taken on trust.
        Measuring first changed the brief twice. <b>Most of the bed is never seen:</b> it is 6 x 5
        behind an 1101 x 624 aperture, so only a 4 x 3 block is in frame, and fourteen of the
        thirty tiles are outside the window at both ends of the scroll — they decode an image each
        and paint nothing. <b>And the panels cover the middle of that window</b>, at 767 to 991px
        against 1101, which makes the prized cells the outer ones rather than the central ones.
        The selection uses your twelve; the four not listed —
        Aladdin, Dinshaw&#8217;s, Mere Ho and Ve Maahi — are dropped from the bed entirely.
      </p>
      <div class="ix__pair ix__pair--3">
        <a class="dr" href="/preview/mosaic-a-placed">
          <span class="mono dr__k">A</span>
          <span class="display dr__n">Placed</span>
          <span class="dr__t">Rank is position. Same bed, same thirty tiles, same grid, same brightness cap, same drift — the only thing that changes is which work sits in which cell. The twelve are laid onto the twelve best-measured cells in rank order, so Diwali takes the cell the aperture and the panels leave most open and Bloody Bubbly takes the weakest cell that is still seen. Verified: ranks 01 to 12 occupy the twelve cells measured at full visibility, twelve distinct works, and all fourteen dead cells confirmed invisible at both ends of the scroll. It is the zero-risk option — one array changes on the home page, no CSS moves, and nothing can regress in the aperture arithmetic that keeps type legible. It also does nothing about the waste: those fourteen tiles still load.</span>
        </a>

        <a class="dr" href="/preview/mosaic-b-fitted">
          <span class="mono dr__k">B</span>
          <span class="display dr__n">Anchored</span>
          <span class="dr__t">Rank is area. The first work takes a 2 x 2 block and the second a 1 x 2 — measured at 4.2 and 2.1 cells — and the other ten take one cell each, so the most-wanted work is four times the size of the ones at the bottom rather than merely better placed. Both anchors sit in the outer columns the panels never reach. Only the sixteen ever-visible cells are populated: twelve tiles, twelve images, twelve distinct works, and fourteen images that can never be seen are simply not requested. A correction is baked into this one — the first build used a 4 x 4 grid on the theory that matching the aperture's proportions would waste nothing, and measuring showed only 4 of 16 tiles fully visible, because four columns makes each 407px and the window fits two and a half. Six columns was already the right number.</span>
        </a>

        <a class="dr" href="/preview/mosaic-c-graded">
          <span class="mono dr__k">C</span>
          <span class="display dr__n">Graded</span>
          <span class="dr__t">Rank is depth. Every tile carries its own brightness, from 0.30 for the first work down to 0.11 for the twelfth, so the favourites catch the light and the rest recede into texture — which is what preferred visibility actually looks like on a wall. Rank drives frequency too: six tiles for the first work down to one for the twelfth, across a denser 7 x 6 field, dealt so no work ever touches itself horizontally or vertically. Verified: max brightness 0.30, nothing above the cap, zero touching duplicates. Because it can only ever go darker, the readability guarantee the page depends on is strengthened rather than weakened for eleven twelfths of the bed. It argues the opposite of B — the bed as texture rather than as pictures.</span>
        </a>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="ix__drafts">
      <p class="mono ix__lbl">Mosaic bed — same bed, three placements</p>
      <p class="ix__txt">
        <b>Awaiting a decision — nothing is applied.</b> These three change <i>only</i> which of
        the twelve works stands in which cell. The bed is the live one declaration for
        declaration: six columns, thirty tiles, the same gap, the same -10% inset,
        brightness(0.30) saturate(0.7) contrast(1.05), the same -8% drift and scale(1.06) —
        verified against the home page's own stylesheet. Where the earlier A, B and C proposed
        new geometry (spans, a different column count, per-tile brightness), <b>D, E and F propose
        none.</b>
        <br /><br />
        The placements come from a new measurement aimed at the actual brief — not where the bed
        is visible, but where it is clear <i>of text</i>. Eight scroll positions, twenty-five
        sample points per tile, each hit-tested to see what sits on top. Twelve cells are ever
        clear, and there are twelve works: one each, exactly. The two obstructions pull opposite
        ways, which is why more than one answer is defensible — the inner columns are inside the
        aperture far more (0.33–1.00 against 0.11–0.33) but sit behind panels far more (0.387
        against 0.13). Each page carries three buttons: hide panels, show cell map, and swap to
        the brightest frames — that last one <b>off by default</b>, so what you judge is the frame
        the live bed shows today.
      </p>
      <div class="ix__pair ix__pair--3">
        <a class="dr" href="/preview/mosaic-d-clear">
          <span class="mono dr__k">D</span>
          <span class="display dr__n">Clear</span>
          <span class="dr__t">Straight down the measurement: rank one takes the cell that is clear of the aperture edge and of text most often, rank two the next, and so on. Diwali lands in cell 15, unobstructed 0.48 of the time; Taskaree in cell 14 at 0.47 — the only two cells on the whole bed inside the aperture at every scroll position. It maximises time unobstructed, which is the most literal reading of the brief. Its weakness is the flip side of the same fact: those two centre cells are the ones a panel covers when a panel is present, behind text 0.75 of the time against 0.25 for the outer columns, so the favourites are clearest overall but the first to go when text arrives.</span>
        </a>

        <a class="dr" href="/preview/mosaic-e-band">
          <span class="mono dr__k">E</span>
          <span class="display dr__n">Band</span>
          <span class="dr__t">A rule you can see rather than a measurement you have to trust. The centre row reads left to right with ranks one to four, the row above takes five to eight, the row below nine to twelve — so the whole top of the list sits in one band across the middle of the aperture, in order, and the bed reads as an arrangement instead of a scatter. It costs a little against D and buys legibility: ranks 1 and 4 land in the outer cells of the centre row, measured clear 0.29 rather than the 0.47 the inner pair gives, so second and third place are better seen than first and fourth. What it buys is a logic that survives a redesign — move the panels or change the aperture and "the strongest band across the middle" still means something, where a list of cell indices would have to be measured all over again.</span>
        </a>

        <a class="dr" href="/preview/mosaic-f-flanks">
          <span class="mono dr__k">F</span>
          <span class="display dr__n">Flanks</span>
          <span class="dr__t">The other half of the trade-off. Ranks one to six run down the two outer columns, which a panel almost never reaches — behind text 0.13 of the time against 0.387 for the inner ones — so at the moments a visitor is actually reading something, the works still showing on either side of the type are the top of the list. That is the strictest reading of "don't hide behind text". The cost is honest and worth stating: those columns are clipped by the aperture while it is still opening, so the favourites are seen less of the time overall, 0.29 at best against 0.48. It is the right answer if the brief means never behind text, and the wrong one if it means the most screen time.</span>
        </a>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="ix__drafts">
      <p class="mono ix__lbl">Mosaic bed — Flanks II, and two more</p>
      <p class="ix__txt">
        <b>Awaiting a decision — nothing is applied.</b> Same live bed as D, E and F, to the
        declaration. Two things are new across all three. <b>The letterboxed frames are cropped:</b>
        Broken Reflections and Diwali Ke Shor Mein carry black bars inside the source files —
        measured at 115/115 of 1080 and 180/115 of 2234, so 21.3% and 13.2% of the frame. Both are
        scaled past the tile edge, and Diwali is shifted as well because its bars are uneven; the
        first factors put the bar edges exactly on the tile edges, which measured as a 0.0 and
        -0.2px margin, so both now carry about 1% more. Verified clear at 1280&#215;800 and at
        375px. <b>The other ten works measure zero bars</b>, including Shiva and Roar.
      </p>
      <div class="ix__pair ix__pair--3">
        <a class="dr" href="/preview/mosaic-g-flanks2">
          <span class="mono dr__k">G</span>
          <span class="display dr__n">Flanks II</span>
          <span class="dr__t">Flanks with the two changes asked for. Ranks one to six still run down the two outer columns a panel almost never reaches — behind text 0.13 of the time against 0.387 for the inner ones — so at the moments a visitor is reading, the works either side of the type are the top of the list. The Shiva Trilogy and Roar have traded cells: Shiva to cell 8, Roar to cell 14. The ranks keep their works and only those two cells exchange, so nothing else about F moves — verified by diffing the two grids. Both letterboxed frames are cropped.</span>
        </a>

        <a class="dr" href="/preview/mosaic-h-weave">
          <span class="mono dr__k">H</span>
          <span class="display dr__n">Weave</span>
          <span class="dr__t">Neither bet, both alternately. The two obstructions pull opposite ways — the outer columns are almost never behind a panel but are clipped while the aperture opens; the inner columns are inside the aperture far more but carry the panels. Clear bets on one, Flanks on the other. This alternates: odd ranks outer, even ranks inner, so whichever obstruction is in play at a given moment, half the top of the list is clear of it. It is the hedge and should be judged as one — no work sits in the very best cell for its rank, rank one takes an outer cell at 0.29 rather than the 0.48 available, and rank two takes that 0.48. What it buys is that the first two works are never both obscured at once, which neither Clear nor Flanks can claim.</span>
        </a>

        <a class="dr" href="/preview/mosaic-i-core">
          <span class="mono dr__k">I</span>
          <span class="display dr__n">Core</span>
          <span class="dr__t">A centre of gravity, rather than a row, a column or a ranked list. The first four works take the 2 x 2 block at the middle of the aperture — cells 8, 9, 14 and 15, which are the four highest-scoring cells that happen to form a contiguous square — the next four ring it in the outer columns, and the last four take the bottom row. The bed reads as strongest at its centre and falling away outward, which is how a wall of frames is actually hung. It is the only one of these whose shape you can see without the cell map: Clear is a list and Flanks is a rule about columns, and both look like a scatter on the page, where a block at the centre reads as a decision even to someone who never learns what the ranking was.</span>
        </a>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="ix__drafts">
      <p class="mono ix__lbl">Mosaic bed — the live one, two works swapped</p>
      <p class="ix__txt">
        <b>Awaiting a decision — nothing is applied.</b> This is the one draft that keeps the bed
        exactly as it runs today: the same sixteen-work cycle in score order, repeating across
        thirty tiles. D through I all replace that cycle with a ranked selection of twelve; this
        changes two entries in the sequence and nothing else. Diffed tile for tile against the
        live home page, <b>three of the thirty cells differ</b> — 13 and 29, where Dinshaw&#8217;s
        becomes Diwali, and 14, where Ve Maahi becomes Aasha. Dinshaw&#8217;s appears twice in the
        cycle, which is why two cells change for one substitution.
      </p>
      <div class="ix__pair ix__pair--2">
        <a class="dr" href="/preview/mosaic-j-live-swap">
          <span class="mono dr__k">J</span>
          <span class="display dr__n">Live, swapped</span>
          <span class="dr__t">The bed that is on the site right now, with Dinshaw&#8217;s replaced by Diwali Ke Shor Mein and Ve Maahi by Aasha, and the two letterboxed frames cropped. The substitutions are made in the sixteen-work sequence rather than in the grid, so each of those two works now appears in the bed twice — once in its own slot and once in the borrowed one — and the swap reads as more of them rather than merely as different neighbours. Both land in cells that are genuinely seen: Aasha takes cell 14, measured clear of the aperture edge and of text 0.47 of the time, which is the second-best cell on the whole bed, and Diwali takes cell 13 at 0.29. Worth knowing before you decide: the single clearest cell, 15 at 0.48, holds Mere Ho — a third swap there would put Diwali in the best spot on the bed.</span>
        </a>

        <a class="dr" href="/preview/mosaic-k-live-swap2">
          <span class="mono dr__k">K</span>
          <span class="display dr__n">Live, swapped II</span>
          <span class="dr__t">J, with Aasha and Mere Ho exchanged as well, so the clearest cell on the bed is not spent on a work that was not asked for. Aasha takes cell 15 — unobstructed 0.48 of the time, the best there is — and Mere Ho takes 14 at 0.47. Diffed against J, exactly two cells move; diffed against the live bed, four. Mere Ho needed a different kind of crop from the other two: it is not letterboxed, it is a 640 square carrying the record&#8217;s own title, and scanning for near-white pixels against a much darker neighbour puts &#8220;mere ho&#8221; at 0.844 to 0.881 of the height and &#8220;HEEYA TIKKU&#8221; at 0.912 to 0.931. So its crop moves the image DOWN rather than up, pushing the type past the bottom edge — the opposite direction from Diwali&#8217;s. The scale is set by the worst case, a square in a portrait tile where the whole frame is in view vertically: measured, the visible band ends at 72.1% on desktop and 79.8% on a phone, both clear of type starting at 84.4%, with no gap opening at the top.</span>
        </a>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="ix__drafts">
      <p class="mono ix__lbl">Mosaic bed — two further swaps on the live bed</p>
      <p class="ix__txt">
        <b>Awaiting a decision — nothing is applied.</b> J is now live, so these two start from
        what the site actually renders and add one exchange each. They import the cast and the
        crops from <i>src/lib/bed.ts</i> — the same tables index.astro renders from, extracted so a
        draft cannot quietly describe an older version of the site. Diffed against the live bed,
        <b>four cells change in each</b>: the two that are seen, and their partners in the ring
        that never is.
      </p>
      <div class="ix__pair ix__pair--2">
        <a class="dr" href="/preview/mosaic-l-crayon-shiva">
          <span class="mono dr__k">L</span>
          <span class="display dr__n">Crayon / Shiva</span>
          <span class="dr__t">My Red Crayon and The Shiva Trilogy exchange — Shiva to cell 7, Red Crayon to cell 8, with cells 23 and 24 trading alongside them where nobody can see it. Measured, cell 8 is clear of the aperture edge and of text 0.175 of the time against cell 7&#8217;s 0.115, so it promotes Red Crayon by about a third and demotes Shiva by the same. Both cells sit in the top row, so neither work reaches the always-visible middle band either way: this is a choice between two similar positions rather than a move into or out of prominence.</span>
        </a>

        <a class="dr" href="/preview/mosaic-m-mereho-moholla">
          <span class="mono dr__k">M</span>
          <span class="display dr__n">Mere Ho / Moholla</span>
          <span class="dr__t">Mere Ho and Moholla exchange — Moholla to cell 9, Mere Ho to cell 10. This one has a reason beyond preference. Mere Ho is the single frame in the bed with type burned into it: a square record sleeve carrying its own title, measured at 0.844 to 0.931 of the frame height, and the live bed does not crop it. Moving it from cell 9 to cell 10 takes it from clear 0.175 of the time to 0.115, so that title is on screen about a third less often, and Moholla — which carries no type — takes the better cell. It is the cheapest way to make the burned-in title less present without cropping the frame at all.</span>
        </a>
      </div>
    </section>

    <ol class="ix__list">
      {options.map((o) => (
        <li class="op">
          <a class="op__head" href={o.href}>
            <span class="mono op__k">{o.k}</span>
            <span class="display op__name">{o.name}</span>
            <span class="op__go mono">Open ↗</span>
          </a>
          <p class="op__line">{o.line}</p>
          {o.ref && <p class="mono op__ref">{o.ref}</p>}
          <div class="op__grid">
            <div class="op__cell">
              <p class="mono op__lbl">The idea</p>
              <p class="op__txt">{o.what}</p>
            </div>
            <div class="op__cell">
              <p class="mono op__lbl">What makes it alive</p>
              <p class="op__txt">{o.live}</p>
            </div>
            <div class="op__cell">
              <p class="mono op__lbl">Judge it on</p>
              <p class="op__txt">{o.against}</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </li>
      ))}
    </ol>
  </div>
</Base>

